BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTENDEE;CN="Daniel Gilbey";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="daniel.gilbey@xcess
 ion.co.uk";ROLE=CHAIR:mailto:daniel.gilbey@xcession.co.uk
ATTENDEE;CN="David Barrow";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="david@itsmpeople.co.
 uk";PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT:mailto:david@itsmpeople.co.uk
ATTENDEE;CN="Sunil Duggal";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="sunil.duggal@xcessio
 n.co.uk";ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT:mailto:sunil.duggal@xcession.co.uk
ATTENDEE;CN="Tim Mills";CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;EMAIL="tim@itsmpeople.co.uk";R
 OLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT:mailto:tim@itsmpeople.co.uk
CREATED:20250722T140027Z
DESCRIPTION:Please join on this link - https://streamyard.com/cd9az9x2td
 \n\nTalk track and background for the conversation.\n\nCustomers are sti
 ll struggling to do basic things well\, and vendors are pushing 'advance
 d' capabilities harder than ever. Some vendors (e.g. SN) are throwing ev
 erything into the gen AI and agentic AI bucket - they see that as the fu
 ture and everyone will get there or die before they do. Others (e.g. Fre
 sh) are saying you can run basic ITIL processes OOTB easily and they wil
 l layer on advanced capabilities in an accessible way. Some (e.g. Halo a
 nd to an extent BMC) are somewhere in the middle - flexibility with adde
 d features (and benefits).\n\nRather than talk about vendors\, what woul
 d be good with would be to talk about starting from a customer perspecti
 ve. How should a customer evaluate it's own requirements? How do they 'k
 now' if they would be better off with Fresh\, Halo or SN? How do they re
 ally decide if they need a customised process and therefore that is a bi
 g influence in how they operate vs changing their model? In other words 
 can we talk about people and processes first\, rather than technology (w
 hich seems to be the way it often happens)?\n\nIntro to the session… Dan
 iel\n\nQ1 – Sunil:\n\n  *   Let’s assume you were talking to a customer 
 who was unhappy with their existing toolset\, and think that they need t
 o make an investment in tooling (that’s not necessarily a change to star
 t off with). How would you help them understand what that investment sho
 uld look like?\n\nQ2 – Dan:\n\n  *   In our hypothetical scenario\, let’
 s now assume that the customer feels that their current tool is not goin
 g to be right for the future. How would you help them understand how to 
 go to market – what should they be evaluating?\n     *   Try and frame i
 n the context of the background if possible\, so should they be looking 
 at ‘simple’ OOTB\, should they be looking at moderate flexibility or sho
 uld they be looking at ‘advanced’ features like agentic AI?\n\nQ3 – Suni
 l\n\n  *   ITSM people offers assessments like this. Can you talk throug
 h some of the specifics of these assessments – who are you talking to\, 
 what sort of questions do you ask them\, and why?\n\nQ4 – Dan\n\n  *   O
 K\, so you have helped assess a customer requirements and define these\,
  you engage with a tooling solution partner like Xcession. How do you wo
 rk with them to guide them to ensure a successful customer solution?\n\n
 Q5 – Sunil\n\n  *   Wrap up. Coming back to the context of the discussio
 n – helping customers make the right decision – what your top 3 do’s\, a
 nd top 3 don’ts\n\n
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20250812T115500
